Title:
RustPack Features (Initial Access, Lateral Movement, Persistence)

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post describes practical red-team use cases for RustPack, a Rust-based packer/loader framework intended to produce payloads that are less likely to be flagged by modern AV/EDR. It focuses on how to operationalize RustPack outputs (EXE, DLL, XLL, CPL, PowerShell, shellcode) for initial access, lateral movement, and persistence when common delivery formats are blocked by MOTW/SmartScreen and gateway controls. Key techniques include custom DLL sideloading with non-public signed binaries (example: Oracle-signed `appletviewer.exe` loading `jli.dll`), conditional execution to avoid sandboxes (domain/AAD-joined checks), and polymorphic shellcode generation for in-memory execution. For lateral movement, it shows packing known offensive tooling (e.g., `nanodump` for lsassy) to reduce signature hits, and combining RustPack with remote execution frameworks (e.g., Titanis/WMI) and COM-hijack-based session targeting (SpeechRuntimeMove). For persistence, it covers DLL sideload persistence (export forwarding/clone of legitimate DLLs) and COM hijacking via HKCU CLSID `InProcServer32`, with process-gating and one-shot mutex behavior to limit noisy callbacks. Useful primarily for red teams/pentesters and operators who need disk-droppable artifacts with reduced static/ML detections.

Technical Focus:
- DLL sideloading & DLL search order hijacking (custom/non-public signed binaries)
- Payload gating: domain/AAD checks, process-name conditions, mutex one-shot execution
- Polymorphic shellcode generation and in-memory execution
- Credential access tradecraft: LSASS dumping workflows (lsassy + nanodump) and PPL considerations
- Remote execution/lateral movement tooling (SMB file copy, WMI/Titanis, DCOM/COM hijack)
- Persistence via export-forwarding “clone” DLLs and COM hijacking (HKCU\Software\Classes\CLSID)

Use Cases:
- Generate sideloadable DLLs for initial access using a signed third-party binary not covered by common detection lists
- Package/obfuscate known offensive binaries (e.g., LSASS dumpers) to reduce signature-based detections when dropped to disk
- Produce shellcode for custom loaders or script-based stagers while relying on polymorphism/anti-analysis features
- Execute implants remotely via signed process execution (copy + run) to reduce SOC suspicion compared to unsigned tooling
- Achieve persistence via application DLL sideloading (export forwarding) or user-level COM hijacking with process-gated execution

Keywords:
RustPack, Rust, packer, loader, AV evasion, EDR evasion, Mark-of-the-Web, SmartScreen, DLL sideloading, DLL hijacking, export forwarding, XLL, CPL, shellcode, polymorphic, sandbox evasion, domain-joined check, Azure AD, LSASS, nanodump, lsassy, PPL, WMI, Titanis, SMB, COM hijacking, InProcServer32, HKCU CLSID, SpeechRuntimeMove, DCOM